nodes.body:
I measured it on current main, which has #64425 (f81a414fae), with Qwen3.8-27B BF16 at TP4 on B300 and three servers per build in alternating order. Folding `in_proj_ba` on top of the side stream raises decode at batch 1 from 231.4 to 235.9 tok/s (+1.9%) without speculation and from 525.0 to 531.7 tok/s (+1.3%) with MTP and 3 draft tokens. At 16 concurrent requests it adds 0.5%. GSM8K (first 500 questions, greedy) stays at 95.0%.

Per GDN layer, in a CUDA graph of 48 layers with cold weights, the input projection at one token takes 14.4 us as two GEMMs, 12.5 us with the side stream and 10.1 us as one fused GEMM. At 4 and 16 tokens the side stream and the fused GEMM are within 0.15 us of each other, and at 64 and 256 tokens the fused GEMM is 3.1 and 3.8 us faster.

@gau-nernst is right that the side stream covers more checkpoints. The fold applies only when both projections are unquantized and `in_proj_ba` is TP-sharded, so I'd keep it as a fast path on top of #64425, with the fused GEMM where it applies and the side stream everywhere else. The fused GEMM rounds the `ba` outputs differently in bf16, so greedy outputs change (9 of 33 prompts match main), while the side stream keeps them bitwise identical.

